The Problem: The Broken Encapsulation

Imagine you are building a Text Editor (like Notepad). You want to save the state of the document so the user can
hit Ctrl+Z.

The Beginner Way:
The Junior Developer creates an external HistoryManager class that reaches directly into the TextEditor to grab its
variables (the text, the cursor position, the font size) and saves them in a List.

Why a Senior rejects this:
This violates Encapsulation (Information Hiding). If the HistoryManager reaches inside the TextEditor to grab its
private variables, it means those variables are no longer private! The TextEditor has exposed its internal guts
to the outside world. If you change how TextEditor stores text internally, HistoryManager completely breaks.
The Senior Solution: The Memento (The Time Capsule)

The Memento Pattern says: "Only the object itself is allowed to take a snapshot of its own internal state."

The TextEditor builds a tiny, sealed Time Capsule (the Memento). It puts its private data inside the capsule, locks
it, and hands the locked capsule to the HistoryManager.
The HistoryManager can hold the capsule, but it is not allowed to open it or look inside. If the user hits "Undo,"
the HistoryManager just hands the locked capsule back to the TextEditor, who is the only one with the key to open
it and restore its own state!